2026-10-17  Daniel Adolfsson <daniel@ashen.se>

    * Unreleased

        * ndppd warns at startup when the upstream and downstream links
          are not separate: an iface rule whose interface is the proxy
          interface, or shares a bridge with it, and a static rule whose
          prefix is routed back out the proxy interface or a bridge-mate
          of it.  This is the usual first attempt on Proxmox, and it fails
          intermittently rather than outright.  From benaryorg's analysis
          in #81.

        * New log level, warning, between error and info.

2026-10-15  Daniel Adolfsson <daniel@ashen.se>

    * Version 1.0.0-rc1

        * ndppd has been rewritten from scratch, and is written in C (C99).
          Some features have been ported over from the 0.2.x branch.  The
          configuration file was reorganised in the process and is not
          portable from 0.2.x; unknown keywords are rejected rather than
          ignored.

        * Packets are now captured in promiscuous mode, so ndppd sees the
          unicast Neighbor Solicitations an upstream router uses to refresh
          an existing neighbor entry.  0.2.x listened on an all-multicast
          socket and never saw them, which is why entries went STALE and
          then FAILED under sustained traffic, and why ndppd appeared to
          work only while tcpdump was running.

        * Numerous RFC 4861 conformance fixes, found in production by
          Toto <https://github.com/totoCZ> and contributed as #93: the
          solicited-node multicast MAC was wrong by one byte; a unicast
          solicitation omitting the source link-layer address option was
          discarded rather than answered; advertisements omitted the
          OVERRIDE flag; the source address of an advertisement is now set
          explicitly instead of left to the kernel; the hop limit 255 check
          on receive (section 6.1.1) was missing; and a socket membership
          was dropped by the wrong process at fork, leaving the daemon
          unable to see the packets it exists to answer.

        * The ICMPv6 checksum is verified on receive, and solicitations
          with a multicast or solicited-node target are discarded.

        * Session state machine corrections around STALE and around routes
          that appear after a session was already created.

        * The experimental "use-kernel" option has been removed.  It
          duplicated proxying to the kernel, which does not handle unicast
          neighbor discovery, and so did nothing but duplicate packets.

        * Support for epoll has been added for better scaling. It's still
          possible to revert to using poll by defining NDPPD_NO_USE_EPOLL.

        * Managing routes and keeping track of local addresses are now done
          through the use of Netlink on Linux and AF_ROUTE for BSD/MacOS.
          Autowire is implemented using this.

        * Support for /proc has been removed.

        * Man pages are now written in .adoc format.

        * The compiled-in default configuration path is now an absolute
          path derived from SYSCONFDIR, rather than a path relative to the
          working directory.

        * A test suite has been added: 228 unit assertions and 55
          integration assertions, both emitting TAP 13, run by "make
          check".  The integration layer drives a real daemon across a
          three-node topology and asserts on what reaches the wire, using
          network namespaces on Linux (requiring no root) and VNET jails on
          FreeBSD.  Continuous integration builds with gcc and clang in
          release and debug, runs the unit suite under AddressSanitizer and
          UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, and runs both layers on Linux and
          FreeBSD.

        * Known limitations at this release: MLD proxying is not
          implemented (#55), and the packet path assumes Ethernet framing,
          so tun, wg and other point-to-point devices are unsupported
          (#72).

2017-01-07  Johnathan Sharratt <johnathan.sharratt@gmail.com>

    * Version 0.2.6

        * Added a new configuration setting named "deadtime" which allows
          sessions that never made it the VALID to have a different (i.e.
          shorter) life before they are removed (and potentially retried)
          (defauilt is the same value as usual TTL for backwards compatibility)

        * Added a new configuration setting named "autowire" in the proxy
          section (default is off)

        * If the "autowire" setting is on, then upon receiving a NDP
          Neighbor Advertisment from one of the rule interfaces, a route will
          be automatically added into the linux IP routing tables thus allowing
          for a full featured gateway when IPv6 forwarding is turned on.
          Note: Be careful as "accept_ra" may need to be set to 2 on the
          interface during testing for the routing tables to retain their
          default route (unrelated to this patch but took me a while to
          discover).

        * When a session ends then anything that was "autowired" will be
          automatically removed thus ensuring the routing tables are in a
          similar state to before the daemon (or session) made any changes

        * Added a feature where the session will attempt to renew itself
          (with a new NDP Solicitation) before it self-terminates, this is
          required otherwise packets could be lost when the session terminates
          triggering the automatically removal of the route table entry.

        * Ensured that renew operations only take place if the session has
          been recently touched by an external solicitation - this ensures
          that sessions that become IDLE are cleaned up quickly

        * Moved the daemonizing step till after the system executed the
          configure step so that the error exit codes are returned to the daemon
          caller.

        * No longer continuing to load the daemon if any of the interfaces fail
          to load which should give a more predictable behaviour and better user experience.

